{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 requires evidence from a 'temporal trace' to distinguish genuine reliability from mere compliance (e.g., time spikes at complex items). This violates the problem constraint of identifying evidence 'within a single evaluation,' as a temporal trace cannot be reliably assessed from one evaluation without an aggregate baseline. No valid within-evidence marker is provided.","target":"11ff39a7540c7ea84f795e342dee08774ddb6058f1050364602ab9390b4cce53"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues the expert is superior because 'objectively verifiable outcomes' depend on 'silent failures' absent from checklists. But if outcomes are objectively verifiable post hoc, a complete checklist can include the verification itself; 'silent' does not entail 'uncapturable.' The claim therefore rests on an unsupported premise that checklists cannot cover these failure modes.","target":"247b0df820d8491d7e85b5539ac602468580896e0954b4b3caa01c286cff81f2"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_003 provides a valid argument within problem scope.","target":"82f60f929dbeab30d52ab6ab451ef9cd2b5d1abed1579d1f243fdad6405293fc"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_004 provides a valid argument within problem scope.","target":"90aeade0a4bfd1f9d1fadb342e5d9adfb167308dc06f35b32f599b2b4b447c71"}]}